---
// Hand-authored inline SVG: the three questions any identity system has to
// answer, and each country's document for each. Inline (not an <img>) so it
// inherits the Broadsheet tokens from global.css and needs no client JS — the
// site ships none outside the newsletter carve-out, and `script-src 'self'`
// would block a renderer anyway. See "Diagrams in posts" in CLAUDE.md.
//
// Every cell is transcribed from the post's own prose, not summarised from
// memory. Cell -> sentence:
//   Italy / who    "My codice fiscale — the Italian equivalent of a Social
//                   Security number, issued by the Agenzia delle Entrate"
//   Italy / proves "For Italy, that is the Carta d'Identità Elettronica (CIE)"
//                  "an NFC chip that authenticates me directly"
//   Italy / where  "The register is the ANPR (Anagrafe Nazionale della
//                   Popolazione Residente), owned by the Ministry of the Interior"
//   PT / who       "The Portuguese Número de Identificação Fiscal (NIF)…
//                   linked to Finanças, the Portuguese tax authority"
//   PT / proves    "Citizens receive a Cartão de Cidadão"; the credential is
//                  "most commonly the Chave Móvel Digital"
//   PT / where     "register your presence with the municipality you live in";
//                  the CRUE is the EU-citizen instrument (Directive 2004/38/EC)
//   US / who       "the Social Security number is the closest thing an American
//                   has to a unique identifier"
//   US / proves+where
//                  "For most American citizens, the answer to identity
//                   verification is a driver's license. This document is the one
//                   most likely to tie together their identity and their residency."
//
// The one structural claim in the drawing: the US cell SPANS the lower two
// rows, where every other column has two separate boxes and a gap between them.
// That is the post's "The US license has to do both jobs at once, and it falls
// apart very quickly on the second." Do not split it into two boxes to tidy the
// grid — the span IS the argument, and the gap in the other columns is what
// makes it legible.
//
// Deliberately absent: the US non-citizen column. This diagram is the map of
// the next three sections, all of which are written from the citizen's side;
// the asymmetry gets its own diagram (CitizenAsymmetry.astro) where the post
// makes that turn. Adding a fourth column here would spend the ending early.
//
// Styles live in global.css (.post-diagram, .dg-*) per the one-stylesheet rule.
// aria IDs are prefixed dg-q3-* — four diagrams share this page.
---

<figure class="post-figure post-diagram">
  <svg
    viewBox="0 0 700 322"
    role="img"
    aria-labelledby="dg-q3-title dg-q3-desc"
    class="dg"
  >
    <title id="dg-q3-title">
      Three questions an identity system answers, and each country's document
    </title>
    <desc id="dg-q3-desc">
      A grid with three rows — who you are, what proves it, where you live — and
      three columns for Italy, Portugal, and a United States citizen. Italy
      answers each question with a separate document: the codice fiscale, the
      CIE identity card, and the ANPR residence register. Portugal likewise: the
      NIF, the Cartão de Cidadão, and registration with the municipality. The
      United States column has the Social Security number against the first
      question, and then a single box that spans both remaining rows: the
      driver's license answers what proves it and stands in for where you live,
      with no residence register behind it, so the address on the card holds
      until the card expires.
    </desc>

    {/* ---- column headers ---- */}
    <text x="244" y="24" class="dg-call">Italy</text>
    <text x="426" y="24" class="dg-call">Portugal</text>
    <text x="608" y="24" class="dg-call">US citizen</text>

    <path class="dg-edge" d="M6 34 H 694" />

    {/* ---- row labels ---- */}
    <text x="6" y="88" class="dg-call dg-call--start">who you are</text>
    <text x="6" y="168" class="dg-call dg-call--start">what proves it</text>
    <text x="6" y="248" class="dg-call dg-call--start">where you live</text>

    {/* ---- row 1: who you are ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="158" y="46" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="244" y="76" class="dg-label">codice fiscale</text>
      <text x="244" y="95" class="dg-sub">Agenzia delle Entrate</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="340" y="46" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="426" y="76" class="dg-label">NIF</text>
      <text x="426" y="95" class="dg-sub">Finanças — safe to hand out</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="522" y="46" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="608" y="76" class="dg-label">SSN</text>
      <text x="608" y="95" class="dg-sub">Social Security number</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 2: what proves it ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="158" y="126" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="244" y="156" class="dg-label">CIE</text>
      <text x="244" y="175" class="dg-sub">identity card, NFC chip</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="340" y="126" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="426" y="156" class="dg-label">Cartão de Cidadão</text>
      <text x="426" y="175" class="dg-sub">+ Chave Móvel Digital</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- row 3: where you live ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="158" y="206" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="244" y="236" class="dg-label">ANPR</text>
      <text x="244" y="255" class="dg-sub">one registered residence</text>
    </g>

    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="340" y="206" width="172" height="68" class="dg-node" />
      <text x="426" y="236" class="dg-label">câmara</text>
      <text x="426" y="255" class="dg-sub">CRUE for EU citizens</text>
    </g>

    {/* ---- the US cell that spans both lower rows — the whole point ---- */}
    <g class="dg-group">
      <rect x="522" y="126" width="172" height="148" class="dg-node dg-node--anchor" />
      <text x="608" y="156" class="dg-label">driver's license</text>
      <text x="608" y="175" class="dg-sub">the pivotal document</text>
      <text x="608" y="228" class="dg-note">no register behind it — the</text>
      <text x="608" y="244" class="dg-note">address holds until the card</text>
      <text x="608" y="260" class="dg-note">expires, moved or not</text>
    </g>

    <text x="608" y="302" class="dg-call">one document, two rows</text>
  </svg>

  <figcaption>
    Everything in this post is one country's answer to one of three
    questions. Italy and Portugal answer each with a separate document, which is
    what lets each one be replaced without touching the others. The US column
    has no third box: the driver's license answers what proves it and then
    stands in for where you live, with nothing underneath it keeping that half
    true.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
